{
    "comments": {
        // symbol used for single line comment
        "lineComment": "#",
    },
    // "<"/">" ARE BACK HERE (user ruling 2026-09-03, after 2.0.2 removed them): losing auto-close
    // on plain "<" broke ordinary typing, and that cost more than the bracket-colouring bug below
    // was worth. Kept OUT of "brackets"/"surroundingPairs" instead -- that is where the colour/
    // auto-insert-">" confusion actually lived (see the comment on "brackets" just below), and
    // auto-closing pairs alone does not drive VS Code's native bracket-match/colourisation, so this
    // does not reintroduce that bug. It does bring back inserting ">" automatically after typing
    // "<", which is a DIFFERENT, accepted trade-off, not the reported bug -- see the "brackets" note.
    // symbols that are auto closed when typing
    "autoClosingPairs": [
        ["<", ">"],
        ["[", "]"],
        ["(", ")"],
        ["\"", "\""],
        ["'", "'"]
    ],
    // "<" AND ">" ARE DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED HERE. Reported 2026-09-03:
    // "<element[true]> <= <element[123]>:" changed the colour of the "[123]" brackets and of every
    // line below it. That came from THIS declaration, not from the TextMate grammar (fixed
    // separately in denizenscript.tmLanguage.json, commit 876c6ee) -- VS Code's native bracket
    // matcher works on raw characters with no lookahead, so it cannot tell a tag-opening "<" from a
    // comparison "<" the way the grammar now can. Denizen reuses "<"/">" as its comparison operators
    // in every "- if"/"- while" condition, so counting them as a guaranteed-balanced pair here is
    // unsound: a bare "<" or "<=" leaves one "<" the native counter never sees closed, and that
    // miscount persists for the REST OF THE DOCUMENT (it is not reset per line, unlike the grammar),
    // which is why it looked like it was "infecting" lines far below the one that was actually
    // typed. Removing the pair here (but not from autoClosingPairs above) means "<"/">" get no
    // native bracket-pair colouring or match-highlight; their only colour now comes from the
    // grammar's single, consistent "keyword.operator.denizenscript" / "meta.tag.denizenscript"
    // scopes, which is what made "==", "<=", ">=" and ">" render as different colours from each
    // other in the first place -- the native layer was painting just the raw "<"/">" characters on
    // top of the grammar's scope, and "==" has neither character to paint.
    // ONLY "[" "]", ON PURPOSE. Reported 2026-10-25, once the palette stopped painting over VS Code's
    // bracket-pair colours (2.5.0 draws it with semantic tokens): "likes: [)|(]" in a data container
    // and "<list[(|)]>" turned red as unmatched. Parentheses and quotes are ordinary text in Denizen --
    // decoration in player-facing strings, an apostrophe in "don't" -- and the native matcher counts
    // raw characters across the whole document, so one stray "(" or "'" marks every bracket below it.
    // Square brackets are tag-parameter syntax and always balanced: the one pair worth colouring.
    // Typing still auto-closes all of them -- that is "autoClosingPairs", above.
    "brackets": [
        ["[", "]"]
    ],
    // NO PER-DEPTH BRACKET COLOURS. Reported 2026-10-26: once the palette became semantic tokens
    // (2.5.0), VS Code's bracket-pair colours -- yellow, pink, blue by nesting depth -- showed
    // through on every "<[x]>" and "tag[...]", which the 2.4.1 palette had painted over in
    // tag_param_bracket. Denizen nests brackets in nearly every tag, so depth colours are noise
    // here, and they made the palette look different from what its users are used to. This list
    // only controls colouring: matching the pair under the cursor still uses "brackets" above.
    "colorizedBracketPairs": [],
    // symbols that that can be used to surround a selection
    "surroundingPairs": [
        ["[", "]"],
        ["(", ")"],
        ["\"", "\""],
        ["'", "'"]
    ],
    // regex for when to auto-indent
    "indentationRules": {
      "increaseIndentPattern": "^(This_Should_Never_Happen){5000}$",
      "decreaseIndentPattern": "^(This_Should_Never_Happen){5000}$"
    },
    "onEnterRules": [
        {
            "beforeText": "^.*:$",
            "action": {
                "indent": "indent"
            }
        }
    ]
}
